% CANON(x): the canonical form of a number -- §4.1's, with the fraction's
% trailing zeros and a bare point removed. Identity in SEL is structural (§5.4):
% DEDUPE, BUCKET, EQL, IN and index keys see 1, 1.0 and 1.00 as three values.
% CANON is how a rule says "compare these by value": the one spelling every
% equal number shares, so structural identity becomes numeric identity.

### name: canon.drops-trailing-zeros
--- note
The fraction's trailing zeros go: the canonical form of a number is the one
spelling every equal number shares (§4.1, §7.6).
--- source
CANON(1.50)
--- expect
num 1.5
===

### name: canon.drops-a-bare-point
--- note
A fraction of zeros goes with its point.
--- source
CANON(2.000)
--- expect
num 2
===

### name: canon.keeps-an-integer-s-zeros
--- note
Only a fraction's zeros are trailing zeros: 100 is not 1.
--- source
CANON(100)
--- expect
num 100
===

### name: canon.integer-with-a-zero-fraction
--- source
CANON(100.00)
--- expect
num 100
===

### name: canon.keeps-inner-zeros
--- source
CANON(10.010) & " " & CANON(1.0203)
--- expect
text "10.01 1.0203"
===

### name: canon.zero
--- source
CANON(0.000)
--- expect
num 0
===

### name: canon.negative-zero-text
--- note
A zero never carries a minus sign (§4.1), whatever the text said.
--- source
CANON("-0.0")
--- expect
num 0
===

### name: canon.negative
--- source
CANON(-1.10)
--- expect
num -1.1
===

### name: canon.leading-zeros-of-text
--- note
Text that is a number gives that number's canonical form, leading zeros
and all.
--- source
CANON("007.50") & " " & CANON("-007")
--- expect
text "7.5 -7"
===

### name: canon.long
--- note
No length limit: the digits are the decimal core's, not a server's type.
--- source
CANON("123456789012345678901234567890.123000")
--- expect
num 123456789012345678901234567890.123
===

### name: canon.small
--- note
Nor a scale limit.
--- source
CANON(0.00000000000010)
--- expect
num 0.0000000000001
===

### name: canon.idempotent
--- source
CANON(CANON(2.50))
--- expect
num 2.5
===

### name: canon.of-sums-and-products
--- note
Equal results of different computations get one spelling: 0.5 + 0.5 is 1.0
and 2 * 0.50 is 1.00 (§4.2), and both are 1 here.
--- source
CANON(0.5 + 0.5) & " " & CANON(2 * 0.50)
--- expect
text "1 1"
===

### name: canon.of-a-quotient
--- note
A quotient already has its minimal scale when exact (§4.3); an inexact one
keeps its ten digits unless they end in zeros.
--- source
CANON(1 / 4) & " " & CANON(1 / 3) & " " & CANON(1 / 8)
--- expect
text "0.25 0.3333333333 0.125"
===

### name: canon.result-is-a-number
--- note
The result is an ordinary number, with the scale of its canonical form:
2.5 * 2 has scale 1 (§4.2).
--- source
CANON(2.50) * 2
--- expect
num 5.0
===

### name: canon.first-child-of-a-list
--- note
The scalar context of every numeric argument (§3.2).
--- source
CANON(LIST(2.50, 9))
--- expect
num 2.5
===

### name: canon.null
--- source
CANON(NULL)
--- expect
error E_NULL at 1:7
===

### name: canon.dedupe-by-value
--- note
The reason CANON exists: DEDUPE compares structurally (§5.4), so equal
numbers spelled differently are different elements. Through CANON they are
one.
--- source
LIST(0.5 + 0.5, 4 / 4, 3 - 2, 1.00) .> MAP(CANON(_)) .> DEDUPE()
--- expect
tree -{"1"=t"1"}
===

### name: canon.dedupe-without-it
--- note
The contrast: the same four numbers, three spellings, three elements.
--- source
LIST(0.5 + 0.5, 4 / 4, 3 - 2, 1.00) .> DEDUPE()
--- expect
tree -{"1"=t"1.0", "2"=t"1", "3"=t"1.00"}
===

### name: canon.bucket-by-value
--- note
A group key through CANON groups equal numbers together, and _K is the
canonical spelling.
--- source
LIST(RECORD("p", 0.5 + 0.5), RECORD("p", 4 / 4), RECORD("p", 1.50)) .> BUCKET(CANON(_["p"]), RECORD("k", _K, "n", COUNT(_)))
--- expect
tree -{"1"=-{"k"=t"1", "n"=t"2"}, "2"=-{"k"=t"1.5", "n"=t"1"}}
===

### name: canon.eql
--- source
CANON(1.0) EQL CANON(1)
--- expect
bool TRUE
===

### name: canon.in
--- source
CANON(1.0) IN LIST(1, 2)
--- expect
bool TRUE
===

### name: canon.index-key
--- note
An index key is the scalar verbatim (§3.3): 1.00 reads key "1.00", and
CANON(1.00) reads "1".
--- source
A = RECORD("1", "one"); A[CANON(1.00)]
--- expect
text "one"
===

### name: canon.sort-is-numeric
--- note
CANON's results are numbers and sort as numbers.
--- source
LIST(10, 9.0, 100.00) .> MAP(CANON(_)) .> SORT()
--- expect
tree -{"1"=t"9", "2"=t"10", "3"=t"100"}
===
